<CourseLayout title="Labs and practical classes" description="Twelve facilitated in-person labs, with equivalent online activities for students who cannot attend." eyebrow="THE COURSE / LABS" wide>
<section class="callout"><h2>Your first lab, step by step</h2><ol><li><strong>Open a week and read its mission.</strong> It names the skill, exact actions and what you will produce. Use the briefing or separate demonstration if the idea is unfamiliar.</li><li><strong>Attend the in-person lab.</strong> Use its supplied apparatus or paper models with the facilitator. Make a prediction, test it and complete the receiving handover. If you cannot attend, use the online Practice activity for the equivalent model-based task.</li><li><strong>Explain and save the attempt afterwards.</strong> Then try Skill check and Transfer challenge, which change the conditions. Save each phase you want to keep before switching.</li><li><strong>Keep your evidence.</strong> Export a backup and assemble the short fieldwork record. Your best ten of twelve weekly records count.</li></ol><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={withBase("/sessions/week-01/")}>Start Lab 1 ↗</a><a class="button" href={withBase("/assessments/fieldwork/")}>What goes in a fieldwork record?</a></div></section>
<p>The six rows in each two-hour timetable are teaching stages: Briefing, Learn, Practise, Test, Transfer and Debrief. The three playable configurations are <strong>Practice</strong>, <strong>Skill check</strong> and <strong>Transfer challenge</strong>. There is no time limit or hidden objective to unlock.</p>
